Z||000000||HYPTNOTiSE||07-02-2000||05:04 PM||||We're at the shop now, it sounded to me like a broken pushrod or a really loose rocker arm so i pulled both valve covers and and didn't find much of anything wrong, i found two slightly loose rocker arms and thats <BR>about it. It doesn't sounds like a manifold leak and they stay black, and dont glow red hot.  It's a click/clacking noise comming from the top of the engine and increases with engine rpm.<P>Mike LeCompte<P>------------------<BR>Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ nitrous||||reg||11
Z||000001||hugh||07-02-2000||06:25 PM||||It still sounds like loose lifters.Did you try to find the noise with the engine running and the covers off?As far as the manifolds glowing,I've seen references to that on other threads.The only things that will make exhaust manifolds glow in my experience are burned exhaust valves , retarded timing,or possibly a lean condition.<p>[This message has been edited by hugh (edited 07-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||HYPTNOTiSE||07-02-2000||06:32 PM||||I've never heard of loose lifters before.  I dont see how they could be loose.  Maybe one isn't pumping up or something, but i dont see how they could be loose.  I was saying that the manifolds dont glow red hot because i was referring to the fact that the exhuast manifolds arent cracked, because red hot manifolds are ususally a sign of cracked manifolds.||||reg||1
Z||000003||hugh||07-02-2000||06:39 PM||||The only things in the valve train that are variable are the lifters.If they don't have the proper preload many people refer to them as loose.They can make the sound that you described.||||reg||1
Z||000004||HYPTNOTiSE||07-02-2000||06:49 PM||||oh so you're saying the vavles are not adjusted properly, thats what i was thinking as well. I'm going to adjust the lash and put everything back together, thats been the general concencous around here.<P>Mike LeCompte||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||07-02-2000||10:05 PM||||I don't know if you're working on a four or a six, but I can tell you, my 2.8 made a noise like that, and I found a spun bearing, and a couple more really hosed. <P>Taiji<P>modified 85 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||Linx||07-02-2000||10:42 PM||||I'm gonna say the same thing...<P>Mine clacked and it <I>positively</I> sounded like it was coming from the <I>top</I> of the engine.<P>but it was a shreded connecting rod bearing. dang.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric||07-02-2000||11:30 PM||||My 3.1 has a more pronounced clicking noise coming from the top of the engine.  In my case it's mostly the lifters.  My machinist, when he was assembling the long block, adjusted the valves so that the hydraulic lifters acted more like solid lifters and, thereby, behave like solid lifters.  Doing this will give a power increase, but there will be tradeoffs in valvetrain wear.||||reg||1
Z||000008||contra||07-02-2000||11:53 PM||||Check that the exhaust manifolds aren't cracked<BR>i know my fiero makes a clicking sound until i get that welded when i have the cash. For now i am using some sort of exhaust cement to fix it||||reg||1
Z||000009||Taijiguy||07-03-2000||08:40 AM||||Actually, another alternative could be a wasted wrist pin, or it could be in that area anyway. You can determine if there's a problem in the bottom or the top by a couple of methods. I did two things:<BR>I have a friend who works at a motor shop(electric) and they have some HIGHLY technical equipment. Among them, a sound analyzier which isolates every sound being produced be a particular piece of equipment, and records their level in reference to each other. It also indicates the frequency. So we were able to attach the probe to the engine, and get an analysis, and pick out the largest spike, since it was the loudest noise. When we compared the frquency of the sound, it turned out to be roughly 1100 time per minute (which is idle for my 2.8) This indicated the bottom, because the valve train only turns at half engine speed, the sound would have shown up with a frequency of around 550 times per minute at idle. You may be able to find a shop that will do this analysis for you, and it only takes a few minutes. (you may have to do some convincing though, since it's not their specialty)<BR>Once we determined that, we verified by pulling the heads and measuring piston free play with a dial indicator, I found two with between .007 and .009 play. (Then I found a crappeed out main as well after tearing it down. What a mess!)<P>Hope all this is useful to someone!<P>Taiji<P>modified 85 GT<BR>(soon to be 4-Barrel GT!)||||reg||1
Z||000010||HYPTNOTiSE||07-03-2000||11:25 AM||||Well we decided to go with a 3.4 liter.  His engine has 200,000miles and everything is gummy inside.  I think that many of the oil passages are blocked off, that and a combination of my buddy running his engine low on oil.  We're going to put a 3.4 in since it's quick and easy. <P><P>------------------<BR>Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ nitrous||||reg||1
